WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.920
It is a very windy day here here in Rochester,
in the south-east of England,

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:06.800
and the only place I could find to film
where my tripod wasn't getting blown over

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.960
was sheltered here by the castle walls,

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:12.080
so apologies that the background isn't
quite as picturesque as it usually is.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:13.200
I've got a story for you.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.760
I actually cut three whole paragraphs from
the start of my script for this.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:19.360
'Cos I was going to explain the history of
what makes a city a city in the UK,

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:21.360
but honestly, it was a bit dull!

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:25.360
And the actual story here is how a
local government tried to save a bit of money

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:29.280
and ended up losing a status symbol
that was nearly 800 years old.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:30.960
The only thing I need to
cover first is that,

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.200
after centuries of
changes to the rules,

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:36.480
the British definition of a city now,
in the 21st century,

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.800
is that a city is a city because
"it's on the government's list of cities".

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:42.800
That's it. That's the whole definition!

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.600
Despite what many people think,
it's got nothing to do with

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:52.560
size, or power, or population, or cathedrals,
or castles, or universities, or history.

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:55.680
Those might be reasons that a city
was put on the list centuries in the past,

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:57.600
but these days the only rule is:

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.360
is it on the government's list?

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:02.000
And new cities get added to the
list because the Queen says so.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.160
Usually when there's some
big national celebration.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:06.800
Sometimes there'll be a competition
and bidding process,

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:09.920
and a few dozen towns and villages
will file paperwork

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.840
saying why they should get "city status".

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:14.960
The decision's actually taken
by government ministers, of course,

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:18.000
the Queen just approves it, but officially:

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.480
the Queen says "that one!",

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:21.520
and that's a city now.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.320
That was a really bad impression
of the Queen.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:28.480
So the people of Rochester
were quite surprised in 2002

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:31.760
when they discovered that Rochester
was no longer on the list.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:33.360
It hadn't been for four years.

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.880
And they were now just living
in a regular town, not a city.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.280
Not because they'd angered the Queen,
although that would have been fun,

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.160
but apparently because someone
forgot to file some paperwork.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.360
Rochester had been a city
since the 13th century,

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.040
when its cathedral was consecrated.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.520
And that made sense when
there was A Cathedral, and

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:51.920
a small town around it,
and then countryside.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:55.680
But by the 1970s, urban expansion
had merged the string of towns

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:57.360
along this bit of the Medway river.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.280
Strood, Rochester, Chatham:

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.000
they were all one big conglomeration,
joined up together.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.640
So in 1974 as part of national reforms,
all those local governments were merged too,

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.200
legally becoming the Borough of Medway.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:12.320
Rochester as an actual legal
Place With A Local Government...

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:13.520
...was no more.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:17.360
But the Queen issued some special paperwork
called Letters Patent that said,

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:21.920
yes, okay, fine, Rochester, the part
that's been a city for 800 years,

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:23.120
that can still be a city.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:25.360
Which would've been fine.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:30.320
A few years later, the local government
then petitioned to extend that city status

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:33.760
to the whole borough,
calling it Rochester-upon-Medway.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:35.680
Great big city. Covers the whole area.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:38.640
And it's not tied to the
old city boundaries any more,

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:41.040
it's tied to the local
government district instead.

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:44.720
Which probably seemed like
a good idea at the time.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:47.920
Until 1998, when districts were
due to be merged again

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:49.520
in another local government reshuffle.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.320
The towns of Gillingham and Rainham
were going to be connected in,

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.560
and the whole area would legally
become known as just Medway.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:58.800
And by that time,
the rules had changed.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:01.920
To stay a city they didn't just need
a bit of paperwork from the Queen.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:05.200
The local council had to
appoint "Charter Trustees",

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:06.960
a group to represent the original city.

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:09.680
The cost was estimated at about £60,000.

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:15.600
Exactly what happened next depends on
who you ask, and when you asked it.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:19.280
No-one seemed to notice at all
until four years after the reshuffle,

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:22.240
when a local historical society
spotted that Rochester...

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:23.840
wasn't on the list any more.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:27.520
In news articles back then, it was put down
to an administrative error.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:31.120
But a few years ago, one party's politicians
dug through some paperwork,

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:32.320
looked through some minutes of meetings,

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:35.280
and blamed the other side
for doing it as a deliberate policy.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.560
A Freedom of Information Request pulled
the minutes from the archives into public view

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:42.800
just a few weeks ago as I record this,
and as far as I can tell they're inconclusive:

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.760
it might be that the council
didn't understand the consequences,

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:47.760
it might have been that
no-one wanted to pay for it

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:49.680
and the buck kept being passed.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:52.960
Now, there's a standard rule of thumb
called Hanlon's Razor:

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:56.800
never attribute to malice what can
be explained by incompetence.

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:59.680
But also, those meeting minutes are written

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:01.920
in exactly the sort of
kind-of-ambiguous way

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:04.560
that I would write if I knew I was
making a risky decision

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:06.880
and I wanted to avoid blame for it later.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:08.560
The truth is probably lost to history,

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:11.680
because no-one is going to remember
what was actually said in those meetings

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:13.360
more than 20 years later.

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:16.960
And even if they did, they probably
won't want to take the blame now.

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:20.720
And now the city status is lost.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:22.320
Rochester can't get it back.

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:25.360
Because Rochester isn't really
a legally defined place any more.

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:27.360
Sure, you can type the name
into a navigation app,

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:28.480
you will get here.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:32.000
There are signs, you could probably
draw some borders with a bit of research.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:36.240
But there's no legal Rochester Council
who could appoint those trustees.

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:38.160
There hasn't been for twenty years.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:40.960
Medway could become a city.
They've tried a few times,

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:43.600
but the Queen hasn't chosen them yet.

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:46.320
And Medway is not Rochester.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:48.640
Does it make any difference?

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:51.840
Legally, no:
village, neighbourhood, town, city,

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.480
none of them have any distinction
in British law any more.

00:04:54.480 --> 00:04:58.880
But one of those words makes the place
sound bigger and more prestigious,

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:00.800
maybe being on the list would affect

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.520
whether a place gets investment
or businesses moving to the area.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:06.400
But it looks like Rochester
will now forever be...

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:10.720
well, arguably?
Not even a town.

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:12.560
Just a neighbourhood of Medway.

00:05:13.440 --> 00:05:17.280
A very very windy
neighbourhood of Medway!

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:23.840
There's a storm coming tomorrow,
it would've been worse.

